/// DECLINE (not a miscompile) — 2026-07-14, seed rcdzc, iter 44. A `String`-returning HELPER (whose body
/// unwraps a `String.at`) whose result is `==`-compared INSIDE A SELF-RECURSIVE LOOP declines with:
///   error: "borrowing op operand has an ownership this backend cannot yet prove"
/// `cdz check` CLEAN; `cdz compile` DECLINES. An honest known-incomplete decline in the
/// `heap_operand_ownership` analysis (backend/wasm/select.rs), NOT a silent wrong value.
///
///   def char-at(s, i) = match String.at(s, i) { Some(c) => c | None => "" }
///   def cnt(s, i, acc) = if i==2 then acc else cnt(s, i+1, acc + (if char-at(s, i) == "a" then 1 else 0))
///   cnt("aa", 0, 0)  →  DECLINE  (want 2)
///
/// SHARP (each necessary):
///   • the char comes from a HELPER that RETURNS the `String.at` result (a `Core::Call` result) — inlining
///     `String.at` directly in the loop arm computes CORRECTLY (returns 2), so it is the returned-String's
///     ownership at the `==` site the analysis can't prove;
///   • the compare is INSIDE A SELF-RECURSIVE LOOP — two SINGLE (non-loop) calls of the same helper
///     compared to a literal compute CORRECTLY (`char-at(s,0)=="a" + char-at(s,1)=="a"` → 2);
///   • let-binding the helper result (`let c = char-at(s,i) in … c == "a"`) declines identically.
///
/// So `heap_operand_ownership` classifies a helper-RETURNED String (owned — a call result transfers out)
/// but the LOOP-TRANSFORMED body's `=` operand path can't yet prove it, and reject-don't-miscompile fires.
/// Same family as the runtime-`String.at`-content-equality fix (which compacts a String.at slice at the
/// PRODUCER) — but that fix is for a String.at USED DIRECTLY; a String.at RETURNED THROUGH A CALL and then
/// compared in a loop is the residual. WORKAROUND: inline `String.at`'s match in the loop arm rather than
/// factoring a `char-at` helper (what `src/strlex.cdz` does). Fix locus: prove/compact the owned
/// call-returned String operand of `value-eq` on the loop back-edge.
///
/// `run` is exported so `cdz compile` reproduces the decline.
def char-at(s: String, i: Int64) = match String.at(s, i) with
  | Option.Some(c) => c
  | Option.None(_) => ""

def cnt(s: String, i: Int64, acc: Int64) =
  if i == 2 then
    acc
  else
    cnt(s, i + 1, acc + (if char-at(s, i) == "a" then 1 else 0))

/// `cnt "aa" 0 0` should count the two 'a's → 2; it DECLINES at compile.
def run() = cnt("aa", 0, 0)

export { run }
